The three auto-fix findings also survive
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. safe-kill-already-gone - bin/fm-safe-kill.sh
   fm-safe-kill.sh:181 refuses with code 4 when the target is already gone, and
   both bin/fm-watch-arm.sh:502 and the standalone migration treat that as "stop
   was never authorized" - so a one-shot watcher exiting during the check window
   turns --restart into a hard `watcher: FAILED`.

2. afk-start-clears-live-buffer - bin/fm-afk-start.sh
   fm-afk-start.sh:144 now clears a live daemon's escalation buffer and then
   execs a daemon that immediately stands down. Removing the lock-unlink left the
   artifact clearing unguarded.

3. health-tree-portability - bin/fm-health-evidence-lib.sh
   fm_health_tree_pids uses GNU-only `ps --ppid`; every other ppid walk in bin/
   uses the portable `-o ppid= -p` form. On macOS - a supported platform - the
   BFS never finds a child and fm_health_tree_size returns a literal 1.
   This one deserves the same care as an ask-user finding: it is a FABRICATED
   OBSERVATION on the exact evidence channel the captain specified must be real,
   where the file's own contract says an unreadable component must be recorded as
   '?' so a comparison can tell "did not move" apart from "could not be seen".
   It also adds a per-pid fork to the watcher's hot poll loop.

