===============================================================================
CAPTAIN'S AMENDMENT TO PART 4, received after the rebase: ONE reset owner
===============================================================================
"Either compose your wedge-escalation reset into upstream's existing turn-end
recovery progression, or prove they are genuinely different seams and document
the boundary; two resetters coexisting is not an acceptable outcome, and the
both-directions mutation proof applies to the composed result rather than to
your half."

The classification called these COMPLEMENTARY. That answer is not sufficient as
it was written: it asserted the separation rather than proving it, and it left
two reset mechanisms live on one seam with nothing enforcing that they stay out
of each other's state.

THE ANSWER, argued from the property rather than convenience: they are genuinely
different seams, and composing them into one function would make things worse.

  upstream  fm_failure_episode_reset
            subject  : the HOME's supervision chain - is anything supervising?
            state    : .turnend-claude-blocks, .claude-autoarm-failure-notified,
                       .claude-autoarm-failure-alarmed
            evidence : fm_watcher_healthy - a verified live identity-matched
                       watcher with a fresh beacon
            consumers: the Claude Stop hook and the turn-end guard

  this lane health_evidence_reset
            subject  : ONE crewmate's pane - is that worker wedged?
            state    : .wedge-escalations-<key>, .stale-since-<key>
            evidence : that pane's own rendered output, process-tree CPU, live
                       descendants
            consumers: the watcher's stale triage

Neither signal can answer the other's question. A healthy watcher says nothing
about whether crewmate X is stuck, and crewmate X computing says nothing about
whether the home is supervised at all. A single function spanning both would be
a dispatcher with two disjoint branches - two mechanisms wearing one name, which
HIDES the boundary instead of making it checkable. That is the failure mode the
one-owner rule exists to prevent, arrived at from the other side.

WHAT ONE-OWNER THEREFORE MEANS HERE, and what the rebuild must deliver:
  1. One owner of the RULE, not two rules. The seam gets a single documented
     reset principle - alarm state is cleared only by positive evidence about
     the alarm's own subject, never by elapsed time - with two named
     applications and one explicitly stated boundary between them.
     Upstream already obeys that rule: fm_failure_episode_reset fires on
     fm_watcher_healthy and never on a timer. So this is a rule the seam already
     has, stated once and applied twice, not a rule this lane is inventing.
  2. The boundary is PROVEN, not documented only. A test drives each reset with
     the other's state present and asserts it is untouched:
       - firing the failure-episode reset with a wedge ratchet present leaves
         .wedge-escalations-<key> and .stale-since-<key> exactly as they were
       - firing the health reset with a failure episode present leaves
         .turnend-claude-blocks and both .claude-autoarm-failure-* records
         exactly as they were
     That converts "genuinely different seams" from a claim into a fixture.
  3. The mutation proof covers the COMPOSED result, four mutants, not two:
       - time alone resets                  (pardons a real slow wedge)
       - nothing resets                     (restores the ratchet)
       - the health reset also clears the failure episode   (crosses up)
       - the failure-episode reset also clears the ratchet  (crosses down)
     Each must break exactly its own test. The last two are the ones that only
     exist because the amendment was made; without them the boundary is
     unenforced no matter how carefully it is written down.

NOT YET BUILT. The rebased tree still carries Part 4 in its pre-amendment form,
including the busy-alarm-reset defect above. The amendment governs the rebuild;
it is recorded here so it survives this round rather than living in a
conversation.
